2. Mathematical Preliminaries

2.1 Control Lyapunov Functions and SOS Certification

Consider a control‑affine system \dot x = f(x) + g(x)u with f(0)=0 and f,g locally Lipschitz. A C^1, proper, positive definite function V:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} is a Control Lyapunov Function (CLF) if there exists a class \mathcal{K}_\infty function \eta such that for all x\neq 0,

\inf_{u} \big[ \nabla V(x)\cdot (f(x)+g(x)u) \big] < -\eta(\|x\|).

When f and g are polynomial, a polynomial CLF V(x) and a polynomial stabiliser u = k(x) can be found by sum‑of‑squares (SOS) programming: one requires that

-\nabla V(x)\cdot (f(x)+g(x)k(x)) - \epsilon V(x) + \tau(x)(c - V(x))

be a sum of squares for some polynomial multiplier \tau(x) \ge 0. This certifies that the sublevel set \Omega = \{x : V(x) \le c\} is forward‑invariant and that x\to 0 asymptotically for all initial conditions in \Omega (Sontag 1989; Parrilo 2000).

2.2 CLF–CBF Quadratic Programs

To enforce multiple state and input constraints simultaneously, one can deploy a CLF–Control Barrier Function (CBF) Quadratic Program. Given a CLF V and barrier functions h_i(x) \ge 0 that define the safe set, at each time instant the controller solves

\min_{u} \|u - u_{\text{nom}}\|^2 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \nabla V \cdot (f+g u) \le -\gamma V,\; \dot h_i \ge -\kappa_i h_i,

where the constraints are affine in u. Feasibility of the QP at all times ensures both stability and safety (Ames et al. 2019).

2.3 Mean‑Field Control and Propagation of Chaos

Consider N exchangeable agents with dynamics ds_i = b(s_i,\mu_t^N,u_i)dt + \sigma dW_i, where \mu_t^N is the empirical measure. Under Lipschitz conditions, the empirical measure converges in probability to the deterministic flow \mu_t of the McKean–Vlasov equation, with the rate O(N^{-1/2}) and exponential concentration bounds (Sznitman 1991; Carmona & Delarue 2018). In a mean‑field control setting, a single central planner (the AGI) solves an optimal control problem for the population distribution; the consistency condition is simply that the macroscopic state equals the push‑forward of the McKean–Vlasov limit, which is automatically satisfied in the limit.

2.4 SIS Epidemic Model Under the NIMFA Approximation

For the SIS process on a weighted graph \mathbf{A}, under the N‑intertwined mean‑field approximation (NIMFA) (Wang et al. 2003; Van Mieghem 2012), the disease‑free equilibrium is globally asymptotically stable if and only if \mathcal{R}_0 = \frac{\beta}{\mu}\rho(\mathbf{A}) \le 1. The NIMFA neglects inter‑node correlations and becomes exact only on complete graphs; on real social networks, it provides a conservative threshold. We therefore use a sufficient condition based on the maximum row sum: \mathcal{R}_0 \le \frac{\beta}{\mu}\max_i \sum_j A_{ij} \le 1 guarantees stability of the disease‑free state, independent of correlations.

2.5 LaSalle’s Invariance Principle

For an autonomous system \dot x = F(x) with a Lyapunov function V such that \dot V \le 0 on a compact invariant set \Omega, every trajectory approaches the largest invariant set where \dot V = 0. This will be used to combine safety and welfare maximisation.

