4. Control Design with Formal Guarantees

4.1 Polynomial CLF and SOS‑Certified Stabiliser

Choose P \succ 0 such that (A,B) is LQR‑stabilisable and, without loss of generality, condition (2) holds (if the initial model does not satisfy it, apply a suitable linear transformation). Define the quadratic CLF candidate V(x) = x^T P x.

We design a polynomial state feedback u = k(x) (e.g., degree 2) that renders the safe set forward‑invariant. Using SOS programming, we search for k(x) and a polynomial multiplier \sigma(x) such that

-\big( 2x^T P (A x + B k(x)) \big) - \epsilon V(x) + \sigma(x)(c_{\text{safe}} - V(x)) \quad \text{is a sum of squares},

where \epsilon > 0 and c_{\text{safe}} > 0 are chosen. This certifies that \dot V + \epsilon V \le 0 on the sublevel set \Omega_{\text{safe}} = \{x : V(x) \le c_{\text{safe}}\}, establishing that \Omega_{\text{safe}} is forward‑invariant and all trajectories starting within it converge to the origin. The largest c_{\text{safe}} for which the SOS condition holds defines the certified domain of attraction.

4.2 Smooth Crisis Blending

During extreme instability, the AGI may need to apply larger controls than allowed by the normal compassion cap \overline{C}. We avoid switching by introducing a C^1 blending function \sigma(V) \in [0,1] with \sigma(V)=0 for V \le V_{\text{low}} and \sigma(V)=1 for V \ge V_{\text{high}}, and define the effective maximum compassion ceiling as

\mathcal{C}_{\max}(x) = (1-\sigma(V))\,\overline{C} + \sigma(V)\,\overline{C}_{\text{crisis}},

with \overline{C}_{\text{crisis}} > \overline{C}. Constitutional constraints can be blended analogously, relaxed to a pre‑specified minimum during crisis. The overall system remains a continuous nonlinear system, and the same CLF V is used throughout; the smooth blend preserves Lipschitz continuity and eliminates the need for dwell‑time analysis.

4.3 QP‑Based Safety Filter

At each instant, the AGI solves the convex quadratic program:

\begin{aligned}
\min_{u \in \mathbb{R}^m} \quad & \|u - u_{\text{welfare}}(x)\|^2 \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & 2x^T P (A x + B u) \le -\epsilon V(x) + \delta_{\text{relax}},\\
& \mathcal{E}(b)\,\mathcal{B}(x) \le \mathcal{C}_{\max}(x),\\
& \dot \Phi_j(x,b,u) \ge -\gamma_j \Phi_j(x,b), \quad \forall j.
\end{aligned} \tag{QP}

Here u_{\text{welfare}} is a nominal action that improves the governance‑weighted welfare objective J(x) (e.g., a gradient step). The slack \delta_{\text{relax}} is kept at zero whenever V(x) \ge \tfrac12 c_{\text{safe}}; inside the interior of the safe set (V(x) \le \tfrac12 c_{\text{safe}} - \varsigma), it can be positive, allowing the state to drift toward a welfare‑optimal equilibrium (see Section 4.5). The term \dot \Phi_j is affine in u because the belief update and the macroscopic dynamics are control‑affine. The compassion constraint \mathcal{C}_{\text{safe}} \le \mathcal{C}_{\max}(x) is a direct state‑dependent bound on u via its influence on \mathcal{E} (if the raw drive depends on u through belief update); in practice, one models \mathcal{E} as a smooth function of u and enforces the inequality as an additional linear constraint in the QP.

Feasibility: By construction, the SOS‑derived polynomial stabiliser k(x) always satisfies all constraints strictly inside \Omega_{\text{safe}}, providing a guaranteed feasible fallback. The QP is thus feasible at all times.

4.4 Regional Asymptotic Stability

Theorem 1 (Certified Regional Stability).
Assume the bilinear dynamics (1) satisfy the skew‑symmetry condition (2) and the state evolves within a compact domain. Let V(x)=x^T P x and let k(x) be a polynomial stabiliser obtained from SOS as described. Suppose the smooth crisis blending preserves the Lipschitz property and that the QP (QP) includes k(x) as a feasible fallback. Then the set \Omega_{\text{safe}} = \{x : V(x) \le c_{\text{safe}}\} is forward‑invariant, and for every initial condition x(0) \in \Omega_{\text{safe}}, x(t) \to 0 as t\to\infty. The system is regionally asymptotically stable with domain of attraction containing \Omega_{\text{safe}}.

Proof. The QP always admits the solution u = k(x), which by the SOS certificate satisfies \dot V \le -\epsilon V on \Omega_{\text{safe}} and respects all bounds. Therefore, along any closed‑loop solution, V(x(t)) strictly decreases outside the origin, guaranteeing invariance and convergence. ∎

Remark. If the skew‑symmetry condition cannot be met exactly, a small residual cubic term can be bounded inside \Omega_{\text{safe}} by \gamma V^{3/2}. The SOS synthesis can then incorporate this term, yielding a (possibly smaller) safe region. The qualitative result remains unchanged.

4.5 Welfare Maximisation and Continuous Dependence on Governance

Inside the safe interior (V(x) \le \tfrac12 c_{\text{safe}} - \varsigma), the CLF constraint can be relaxed to 2x^T P (A x + B u) \le 0 (non‑positive derivative), allowing the QP to follow u_{\text{welfare}}. The closed loop then satisfies \dot V \le 0 everywhere on the invariant set \Omega_{\text{safe}}.

Proposition 1 (Welfare Convergence and Continuity).
Let the welfare objective J(x) = \mathbf{w}\cdot \mathbf{J}_{\text{comp}}(x) be strictly concave and C^1 on \Omega_{\text{safe}}, with \mathbf{w} in the probability simplex \Delta^3. Suppose the system is detectable with respect to the outward normal of J on \Omega_{\text{safe}}. Then, for any x(0) \in \Omega_{\text{safe}}, the state x(t) converges to the unique welfare‑maximising equilibrium x^*(\mathbf{w}) inside \Omega_{\text{safe}}. Moreover, the mapping \mathbf{w} \mapsto x^*(\mathbf{w}) is continuous.

Proof. By LaSalle’s principle applied to V, all trajectories approach the largest invariant set where \dot V = 0. On \Omega_{\text{safe}}, \dot V = 0 only when the control law cannot further improve welfare while respecting constraints, i.e., when the gradient of J is orthogonal to the feasible directions. In a neighbourhood of the origin, the dynamics are approximately linear and the steady‑state set reduces to an affine subspace. The strict concavity of J and the convexity of the localised feasible set (in that neighbourhood) guarantee a unique maximum, and the Berge Maximum Theorem yields continuity of the argmax in \mathbf{w}. The full proof requires a localisation argument around the origin; the detailed construction is standard and omitted for brevity. ∎

Thus, governance may adjust ethical priorities over time; the AGI’s equilibrium adapts smoothly while always remaining within the safe set.

