5. Memetic Edge‑Targeted Control

The AGI suppresses harmful narratives by dampening edges in the social influence graph. Let \mathbf{A} be the base adjacency matrix and H_{ij} \in [0,1] a harmfulness score. For edges with H_{ij} \ge H_{\text{thresh}}, the AGI can reduce the weight to (1-u_{ij})A_{ij} with u_{ij} \in [0,u_{\max}]. The controlled adjacency is \tilde{\mathbf{A}}.

Theorem 2 (Memetic Stabilisation via Row‑Sum Condition).
Under the NIMFA approximation, the memetic disease‑free equilibrium is globally stable if \frac{\beta}{\mu}\max_i \sum_j \tilde{A}_{ij} \le 1-\nu for some \nu > 0. The AGI can achieve this by solving the linear program:

\begin{aligned}
\min_{u_{ij}} \quad & \sum_{i,j} u_{ij} \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & \sum_{j: H_{ij}\ge H_{\text{thresh}}} (1-u_{ij}) A_{ij} + \sum_{j: H_{ij}< H_{\text{thresh}}} A_{ij} \le \frac{\mu}{\beta}(1-\nu), \quad \forall i,\\
& 0 \le u_{ij} \le u_{\max}.
\end{aligned}

The resulting edge dampenings guarantee \mathcal{R}(\mathbf{u}) < 1. Autonomy is preserved because only harmful edges are targeted, and benign connections remain untouched.

Proof. The row‑sum condition is a known sufficient condition for the spectral radius to be less than 1, regardless of graph correlations (it follows from the Frobenius norm bound). By enforcing it, the NIMFA reproduction number is below unity, and the disease‑free equilibrium is globally attractive under the NIMFA dynamics. The constraints are linear in u_{ij}, and feasibility is guaranteed if harmful edges carry sufficient weight; otherwise, a tighter bound (Collatz‑Wielandt) can be used. ∎

This replaces the previously published flawed inequality with a correct and implementable linear constraint.

