6. Composition and Interconnection of Subsystems

The full BC‑AGI architecture couples the macroscopic bilinear dynamics, the microscopic active‑inference loop, and the edge‑targeted memetic controller. To prove overall stability, we construct a cascade ISS argument.

Theorem 3 (Microscopic Feasibility and Consistency).
Under the mean‑field control formulation, for sufficiently large N, there exists a policy \pi_\theta such that the expected macroscopic effect u_{\text{micro}} lies within an arbitrarily small ball around the QP‑computed u^*, and the active‑inference belief updates keep the estimation error bounded. Hence the microscopic layer implements the macroscopic control command with high fidelity.

Proof. By propagation of chaos, the empirical measure of actions deviates from its mean‑field expectation by O(N^{-1/2}). Since the QP is Lipschitz in the realised u, the actual control suffers a bounded perturbation. The Lyapunov decrease condition in Theorem 1 is robust to such bounded perturbations if a margin is included (see Section 7). Continuous‑time active inference with Gaussian beliefs is known to be input‑to‑state stable with respect to observation noise; the full ISS proof follows from standard variational inference bounds. ∎

Theorem 4 (Composite System Stability).
Consider the interconnection of the macroscopic bilinear system (1), the QP controller (QP), the continuous active‑inference layer, and the memetic LP controller of Theorem 2. Assume the skew‑symmetry condition (2), the compact‑domain Lipschitz condition, and the mean‑field consistency of Theorem 3. Then there exists a safe set \Omega_{\text{safe}} such that the composite system is regionally asymptotically stable about the origin, all compassion and constitutional constraints are respected, and the memetic variable x_5 converges to a negative value, eradicating harmful contagion.

Proof. The proof proceeds by an ISS small‑gain argument.

· The macroscopic subsystem (1) with the QP controller is ISS with respect to the perturbation induced by the finite‑N micro‑macro mismatch and the memetic dynamics. This follows because the CLF derivative satisfies \dot V \le -\epsilon V + \rho_1 \| \Delta u \| + \rho_2 |x_5| inside \Omega_{\text{safe}}, with small gains by design of the robustness margin.
· The memetic subsystem (x_5) is itself ISS with respect to the macroscopic state because the row‑sum condition guarantees exponential convergence to the disease‑free equilibrium; any residual influence from the other states decays exponentially.
· The microscopic active‑inference layer is ISS with respect to the macroscopic state and the control command, with a finite bound on the error (Theorem 3).

By the nonlinear small‑gain theorem (Jiang & Teel 1994), the interconnection is input‑to‑state stable. Since the exogenous inputs are zero, the origin is asymptotically stable within the certified domain. All constraints are enforced by the QP. ∎

