7. Finite‑Population Robustness and Practical Stability

The mean‑field limit is an idealisation; in any real deployment with finite N, the empirical macro‑control u deviates from the McKean–Vlasov prediction. Using standard propagation‑of‑chaos estimates, for any finite horizon T,

\mathbb{P}\!\left( \sup_{0\le t \le T} \|u_{\text{emp}}(t) - u_{\text{ideal}}(t)\| \ge \varepsilon \right) \le C_1 e^{-C_2 N \varepsilon^2}.

We incorporate a robustness margin: in the QP, relax the CLF constraint by adding a constant \Delta chosen such that with probability 1-\delta, \dot V remains negative despite the deviation. By choosing N sufficiently large, \Delta can be made arbitrarily small. The safe set is slightly inflated to \Omega'_{\text{safe}} = \{V \le c_{\text{safe}} + \eta\} with \eta proportional to \Delta. The system then exhibits practical stability: with probability 1-\delta, the state never leaves a small neighbourhood of \Omega_{\text{safe}}, and the neighbourhood shrinks as N\to\infty. For civilisation‑scale AGI (N\sim 10^9), the probability of a significant deviation is astronomically small.

