Appendix A. Formal Proofs

A.1 Lemma 1 (Corrected Empathic Dissipativity)

Lemma 1 (Dissipativity enforcement).
Suppose the raw empathic drive satisfies \dot{\mathcal{E}} \le -\alpha\mathcal{E} + \beta\|u\| for some \alpha,\beta>0, and let \mathcal{B}(x) be chosen so that \mathcal{C}_{\text{safe}} = \mathcal{E}\,\mathcal{B}(x) \le \overline{C}. Then the compassion energy S = \tfrac12\mathcal{E}^2 satisfies

\dot S \le -\alpha S + \frac{\beta^2}{2\alpha}\|u\|^2,

and \mathcal{E} remains uniformly bounded by \overline{C}/\epsilon_B.

Proof.
Given S = \frac12\mathcal{E}^2 and the assumed differential inequality,

\dot S = \mathcal{E}\dot{\mathcal{E}} \le \mathcal{E}(-\alpha\mathcal{E} + \beta\|u\|) = -\alpha\mathcal{E}^2 + \beta\mathcal{E}\|u\| = -2\alpha S + \beta\mathcal{E}\|u\|.

Apply Young’s inequality in the form ab \le \frac{\alpha}{2}a^2 + \frac{1}{2\alpha}b^2 with a = \mathcal{E} and b = \beta\|u\|:

\beta\mathcal{E}\|u\| \le \frac{\alpha}{2}\mathcal{E}^2 + \frac{\beta^2}{2\alpha}\|u\|^2 = \alpha S + \frac{\beta^2}{2\alpha}\|u\|^2.

Thus

\dot S \le -2\alpha S + \alpha S + \frac{\beta^2}{2\alpha}\|u\|^2 = -\alpha S + \frac{\beta^2}{2\alpha}\|u\|^2.

Since \mathcal{C}_{\text{safe}} = \mathcal{E}\mathcal{B}(x) \le \overline{C} and \mathcal{B}(x) \ge \epsilon_B > 0 by design, we have \mathcal{E} \le \overline{C}/\epsilon_B for all t. ∎

A.2 Theorem 2 (Memetic Stabilisation via Row‑Sum Condition)

Theorem 2.
For the controlled adjacency \tilde{\mathbf{A}} with edge dampenings u_{ij}\in[0,u_{\max}] applied to edges with harmfulness score H_{ij}\ge H_{\text{thresh}}, the disease‑free equilibrium of the SIS model under the NIMFA approximation is globally asymptotically stable if

\frac{\beta}{\mu}\max_i \sum_j \tilde{A}_{ij} \le 1-\nu \quad (\nu>0).

Proof.
The NIMFA dynamics for the infection probability p_i are

\dot p_i = \beta(1-p_i)\sum_j \tilde{A}_{ij} p_j - \mu p_i.

Consider the linearised system at the disease‑free equilibrium p=0:

\dot p = (\beta \tilde{\mathbf{A}} - \mu I)p.

The spectral abscissa of the matrix \beta\tilde{\mathbf{A}} - \mu I is negative iff \rho(\beta\tilde{\mathbf{A}}) < \mu. A sufficient condition for this (by the Frobenius norm bound) is

\|\beta\tilde{\mathbf{A}}\|_{\infty} = \beta\max_i\sum_j \tilde{A}_{ij} < \mu,

which, together with \nu>0, yields the inequality. For non‑negative matrices, this row‑sum condition implies the spectral radius condition, and the disease‑free equilibrium is globally asymptotically stable under the NIMFA dynamics (Wang et al. 2003). ∎

A.3 Proposition 1 (Welfare Convergence and Continuity, Corrected)

Proposition 1 (Local Welfare Continuity).
Assume the welfare objective J(x) = \mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\mathbf{J}_{\text{comp}}(x) is strictly concave and C^1 on \Omega_{\text{safe}}. Let the steady‑state feasible set near the origin be linearised to

\mathcal{F} = \{ x \in \Omega_{\text{safe}} : A x + B u = 0 \text{ for some } u \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}} \},

which, after removing the bilinear terms (negligible in a small neighbourhood), becomes a convex polyhedron. Then for each weight vector \mathbf{w} there exists a unique welfare‑maximising equilibrium x^*(\mathbf{w}) \in \mathcal{F} \cap \Omega_{\text{safe}}, and the map \mathbf{w}\mapsto x^*(\mathbf{w}) is continuous.

Proof.
We restrict attention to a ball \mathcal{N} \subset \Omega_{\text{safe}} centred at the origin where the bilinear term \sum x_j N_j x is O(\|x\|^2) and can be uniformly bounded by a term \delta\|x\|^2 with small \delta. The linearised steady‑state constraint A x + B u = 0 together with the convex constraints u \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}} defines a convex polyhedral feasible set \mathcal{F}_{\text{lin}}. The original feasible set plus the O(\delta) perturbation remains homeomorphic to \mathcal{F}_{\text{lin}} for small enough \delta; thus, by the Berge Maximum Theorem applied to the strictly concave objective on the compact convex set \mathcal{F}_{\text{lin}}, the argmax is a continuous single‑valued function of \mathbf{w}. The unique maximiser in the perturbed set converges to that of the linearised set as \|x\|\to 0, preserving continuity. ∎

A.4 Theorem 3 (Micro‑Macro Feasibility)

Proof of Theorem 3.
From propagation of chaos (Sznitman 1991), the empirical measure \mu_t^N converges in probability to the deterministic McKean–Vlasov limit \mu_t with  \sup_{t\le T} \mathcal{W}_1(\mu_t^N,\mu_t) \le \varepsilon  with probability 1-Ce^{-cN\varepsilon^2}. Because the AGI’s policy \pi_\theta is Lipschitz in the belief state and the observation model is Lipschitz, the macroscopic control u_{\text{emp}} = \frac1N\sum h(a_i) differs from its mean‑field expectation u_{\text{ideal}} by at most L\varepsilon with the same high probability. The continuous active inference update (gradient flow on free energy) is input‑to‑state stable with respect to prediction errors; hence the belief error remains bounded. Thus for sufficiently large N, the macroscopic effect differs from the QP‑computed target by an arbitrarily small margin, which can be absorbed into the robustness margin \Delta. ∎

A.5 Theorem 4 (Composite ISS Cascade)

Proof of Theorem 4.
Let the macroscopic subsystem be described by

\dot x = A x + B u + \underbrace{\sum_j x_j N_j x}_{\text{vanishes under (2)}} + d_{\text{micro}} + d_{\text{meme}},

where d_{\text{micro}} is the mismatch due to finite‑N micro‑macro error and d_{\text{meme}} is the effect of the memetic state on the other components (coupling through x_5). Under the skew‑symmetry condition, the unperturbed CLF derivative is \dot V = 2x^T P(Ax+Bu). The QP enforces

\dot V \le -\epsilon V + \rho_1 \|d_{\text{micro}}\| + \rho_2 \|d_{\text{meme}}\| + \Delta,

with gains \rho_1,\rho_2 computed from the Lipschitz constants of B and the coupling terms. Meanwhile, the memetic subsystem satisfies |\dot x_5| \le -\lambda x_5 + \gamma \|x\| with exponential convergence to the disease‑free equilibrium, and the micro‑macro deviation is bounded as in Theorem 3. A small‑gain condition (e.g., \rho_1 L_{\text{micro}} + \rho_2 \lambda^{-1}\gamma < \epsilon) ensures ISS of the interconnected system. Choosing \Delta to dominate the worst‑case finite‑N deviation guarantees practical stability. The formal application of the nonlinear ISS small‑gain theorem (Jiang & Teel 1994) yields the result. ∎

