Appendix B. Sum‑of‑Squares Certification Details

B.1 SOS Formulation for Polynomial CLF

We seek a quadratic Lyapunov function V(x)=x^T P x with P \succ 0 satisfying (2), and a polynomial state feedback k(x) of degree d (e.g., d=2) such that

\dot V(x) = 2x^T P (A x + B k(x)) \le -\epsilon V(x) \quad \forall x\in\Omega_{\text{safe}}.

Introduce a polynomial multiplier \sigma(x) that is SOS. The condition

-\big(2x^T P (A x + B k(x))\big) - \epsilon V(x) + \sigma(x)(c_{\text{safe}} - V(x)) \in \Sigma[x],

where \Sigma[x] denotes the set of SOS polynomials, is sufficient because for any x with V(x)\le c_{\text{safe}}, the term \sigma(x)(c_{\text{safe}}-V(x)) is non‑negative, making the whole expression non‑negative, which implies -\dot V - \epsilon V \ge 0.

B.2 Solving the Bilinear SOS Problem

The variables are the coefficients of k(x) and \sigma(x). The condition is linear in these coefficients once P and a basis are chosen, but the product P B k(x) is bilinear if we also vary P. To circumvent this, we first fix P by solving an LQR problem with a desired gain K_0, ensuring stabilisability and the skew‑symmetry condition. Then k(x) = K_0 x + k_{\text{quad}}(x) where k_{\text{quad}}(x) is a purely quadratic vector polynomial. The SOS problem becomes linear in the unknown coefficients of k_{\text{quad}} and \sigma and can be solved with YALMIP and MOSEK.

B.3 Obtaining the Largest Safe Region

To maximise the domain of attraction, we perform a line search on c_{\text{safe}} while maintaining the SOS condition. The largest feasible c_{\text{safe}} defines the certified invariant set \Omega_{\text{safe}}.

