Appendix C. Mean‑Field Control and Finite‑N Bounds

C.1 Model and Assumptions

Each agent’s state s_i \in \mathbb{R}^d follows

ds_i = \big(f_h(s_i) + g_h(s_i)\,a_i\big)dt + \sigma_h dW_i,

where a_i = \pi_\theta(\mu_i, b_{\text{AGI}}) is the AGI’s action. The feature map \phi:\mathbb{R}^d\to\mathbb{R}^5 and the effect map h:\mathbb{R}^p\to\mathbb{R}^m are Lipschitz with constants L_\phi, L_h, and bounded. The AGI’s policy \pi_\theta is Lipschitz in the belief state \mu_i.

Under these conditions, the empirical measures \mu_t^N satisfy the McKean–Vlasov propagation of chaos: for any finite horizon T, there exist constants C,c>0 depending on the Lipschitz constants such that for all \varepsilon>0,

\mathbb{P}\!\left(\sup_{t\le T} \mathcal{W}_1(\mu_t^N,\mu_t) \ge \varepsilon \right) \le C \exp(-c N \varepsilon^2).

(See Carmona & Delarue 2018, Vol. I, Theorem 2.12.)

C.2 Control Error Bound

Because h is Lipschitz and bounded, the macroscopic control u_{\text{emp}} = \frac1N\sum h(a_i) satisfies

\|u_{\text{emp}} - u_{\text{ideal}}\| \le L_h\, \mathcal{W}_1(\mu_t^{N,\text{action}}, \mu_t^{\text{action}}) \le L_h L_\pi\, \mathcal{W}_1(\mu_t^N, \mu_t),

where L_\pi is the Lipschitz constant of the policy from belief to action distribution. Therefore the same concentration bound applies to u_{\text{emp}} with a modified constant. The robustness margin can be set as \Delta = L_{\dot V} \varepsilon where L_{\dot V} is the Lipschitz constant of the Lyapunov derivative with respect to u.

