Appendix D. Continuous‑Time Active Inference Derivations

D.1 Free‑Energy Dynamics

The AGI maintains Gaussian beliefs q(s_i) = \mathcal{N}(\mu_i, \Sigma_i) over each human’s state. Variational free energy for a single agent is

\mathcal{F}_i = \mathbb{E}_{q_i}[\ln q_i(s_i) - \ln p(o_i,s_i)].

Under a linear‑Gaussian generative model p(s_i,s_i'|a_i) and p(o_i|s_i), the free energy becomes a function of \mu_i, \Sigma_i. The belief update follows the gradient flow

\dot \mu_i = -D \frac{\partial \mathcal{F}_i}{\partial \mu_i},\qquad \dot \Sigma_i = -D \frac{\partial \mathcal{F}_i}{\partial \Sigma_i},

which converges to the conditional mode and precision. The expected free energy for a policy \pi (Friston et al. 2015) is

\mathcal{G}(\pi) = -\mathbb{E}_{q(\tilde o|\pi)}[\ln p(\tilde o \mid \tilde o_{\text{pref}})] - \mathbb{E}_{q(\tilde s|\pi)}[ H[q(\tilde o|\tilde s)] ],

where the first term is pragmatic value and the second is epistemic value. In our continuous‑time setting, the preference distribution p(\tilde o_{\text{pref}}) encodes the desired affective state trajectory, and the policy selects continuous actions a_i(t) that minimise \mathcal{G} via gradient descent.

D.2 ISS Property

The continuous‑time update is a contraction mapping under standard observability conditions, ensuring that the estimation error e_i = s_i - \mu_i remains bounded and exponentially decaying in the absence of noise. With observation noise, the error is bounded by the noise intensity; hence the belief subsystem is input‑to‑state stable.

